# light-tools log roots
#
# Copy this file to the light-tools XDG config directory, alongside config.toml:
#
#   ~/.config/light-tools/.env
#
# It is read from THERE ONLY. A .env in the process working directory is
# deliberately ignored: that tree is agent-writable, so a repo-local file must
# never be able to widen the filesystem boundary light_ops reads within.

# Extra roots that caller-supplied light_ops log paths may be read from.
# Separate entries with the OS path list separator (":" on Linux/macOS).
# A leading ~ is expanded. Every entry listed here is EXPLICIT: if one does not
# exist, startup fails loudly rather than silently narrowing what is readable.
#
# LIGHT_TOOLS_LOG_ROOTS=/var/log:/srv/myapp/logs

# Precedence, highest first:
#   1. LIGHT_TOOLS_LOG_ROOTS in the process environment (set by your launcher)
#   2. LIGHT_TOOLS_LOG_ROOTS in ~/.config/light-tools/.env
#   3. log_roots = ["...", "..."] in ~/.config/light-tools/config.toml
#   4. built-in defaults: /var/log, ~/.local/log, ~/.pm2/logs
#
# The built-in defaults are OPTIONAL — any that do not exist on this machine are
# dropped. Roots you configure yourself are not: a typo is an error, not a
# silent narrowing.
#
# Note that log_roots widens CALLER-SUPPLIED paths only. Logs discovered through
# the service registry (journalctl, docker, pm2) are read wherever the registry
# says they live and are never checked against these roots — confining them
# would break reading service logs, which is the tool's whole purpose.

# The filesystem boundary for light_file and light_bash is separate and is NOT
# settable here. It lives in config.toml:
#
#   allowed_roots = ["/Git/my-project"]

# Outbound terse formatting is a launcher-only process option, not configuration
# authority read from this file. Set it on the MCP server process when desired:
#
#   LIGHT_TERSE_OUTPUT=1 light-tools
#
# Unset and every value other than exactly "1" preserve raw JSON tool text.